WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.120
How do semaphores block and

2
00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.880
wake processes? A semaphore coordinates

3
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.960
access through wait and signal,

4
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.120
with atomic state changes and

5
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.380
a defined waiting discipline. The

6
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.620
blocking implementation in these slides

7
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.560
stores both an integer value

8
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.260
and a list of sleepers.

9
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.840
Wait first decrements the value.

10
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.680
If the new value is

11
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.520
negative, the caller joins the

12
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.600
wait list and blocks; otherwise

13
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.140
it has acquired a permit.

14
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.500
Starting from one, P zero

15
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.100
waits and changes the value

16
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.740
to zero, acquiring the single

17
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:33.680
available permit.

18
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.460
P one now waits, taking

19
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:37.880
the value from zero to

20
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.580
minus one. Because the new

21
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.200
value is negative, it is

22
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.020
enqueued and becomes waiting rather

23
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.660
than consuming processor time in

24
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.200
a spin loop. P zero

25
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.400
remains the permit holder.

26
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.320
If P two also waits,

27
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.220
the value becomes minus two

28
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.480
and the wait list contains

29
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.000
two sleepers. In this signed-value

30
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.860
representation, the negative magnitude describes

31
00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.980
queued waiters. It is not

32
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.720
the same representation as an

33
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.340
always-nonnegative count of free resources.

34
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.420
Signal increments the value and

35
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.740
wakes one waiter when the

36
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.080
new value is less than

37
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.100
or equal to zero. P

38
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.100
zero signaling from minus two

39
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.080
produces minus one, so P

40
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.440
one can leave the wait

41
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.960
list and become ready. Waking

42
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.420
a process does not mean

43
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:22.680
it instantly runs on the

44
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.180
processor.

45
00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.500
With the direct handoff rule

46
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.320
illustrated in the photos, the

47
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.900
awakened waiter has a reserved

48
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.200
permit. A newcomer cannot steal

49
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.880
that permit merely by reaching

50
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.560
the processor first. Dispatch determines

51
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.480
when a process runs, while

52
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.420
the protected counter and waiting-list

53
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.900
rules determine ownership.

54
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.240
All counter checks and queue

55
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.920
coordination must be synchronized. If

56
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.300
one process decrements, another decrements

57
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.300
again, and both later test

58
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.180
the changed shared value, the

59
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.680
first can block incorrectly. Making

60
00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.980
only one arithmetic instruction atomic

61
00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.440
is not enough to protect

62
00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.560
the complete protocol.

63
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.700
The photographed quiz intentionally changes

64
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.300
signal to wake only when

65
00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.440
the new value is less

66
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.500
than zero. Starting at minus

67
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.840
one, signal increments to zero

68
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.840
but then wakes nobody, stranding

69
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.740
the remaining waiter. The correct

70
00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.180
test is less than or

71
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.420
equal to zero after incrementing.

72
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.560
A priority queue or last-in-first-out

73
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.900
list can still starve a

74
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.180
waiter even after that bug

75
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.500
is fixed. Correct wakeup accounting

76
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.420
and fairness are separate properties.

77
00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.260
A signal that happens before

78
00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:30.320
a wait can leave a

79
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:31.540
permit stored in the value,

80
00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.280
rather than vanishing like an

81
00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:34.220
unremembered event.

82
00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:35.980
Things to take note of.

83
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.420
Decrement before the negative test,

84
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.180
increment before the nonpositive wakeup

85
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.820
test, and track the wait

86
00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.860
list with the value. Preserve

87
00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:46.500
the exact buggy code when

88
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.740
tracing a question, then explain

89
00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.840
the correction; do not silently

90
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.480
fix it before computing the

91
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:53.300
requested states.
